description:
Problem: Our current bucketing/coalescer strategies allocate the same per-request pad budget across sequences regardless of kernel sensitivity and attention sparsity. This wastes token compute and increases per-token cost for mixed-length workloads (long-tail user inputs).

Goal: Implement a differential pad budgeting layer that assigns a per-slot pad allowance based on kernel affinity, attention sparsity signal, and prefill vs decode phase, combined with a hierarchical bucketizer that groups requests into multi-tier buckets (fast-path low-pad, medium-pad, slow-path high-pad) to reduce aggregate padding without increasing p99 latency. The design aims to reduce token padding by >=10% on real traffic distributions while keeping 99th- and 95th-percentile latency within SLOs.

Scope: runtime-level scheduler + packing heuristics + kernel hint propagation. Work includes:
- pad-budget estimator that consumes recent seqlen histogram, KV cache hit/miss, and attention-density predictors to set slot budgets;
- hierarchical bucketizer that performs a coarse pass (3 tiers) then fine-grained packing inside each tier to maximize kernel-friendly groupings;
- prefill-aware prioritization: prefill requests are routed with stricter pad budgets; decode requests can accept slightly higher latency but lower padding if they share KV;
- kernel hinting: expose bucket metadata to kernel selector so it can prefer smaller memory kernels for low-pad buckets;
- safety fallbacks and canary flags for rollout.

Trade-offs considered:
- Aggressive pad trimming reduces batch fill and could lower throughput on homogeneous long-seq workloads — mitigated by a feedback loop that loosens budgets if GPU utilization drops below threshold.
- More buckets increase scheduling overhead; chosen 3-tier approach balances overhead and pad savings.
- Kernel hinting requires a stable mapping between bucket and kernel; we add a constraint solver in the scheduler that prioritizes kernel affinity without hard-binding requests.
